//! check: run(exit=trap(exclusivity))
//! phase: run
//! conforms: conf.trap.map, mem.iter.excl, std.list
//!
//! **The cross-implementation triage case, and it AGREES now** (F-0014,
//! closed at the sc11 pin). Pushing to a list while iterating it is
//! `exclusivity` — the trap `[conf.trap.map]` predicted all along — under
//! the interpreter, and a static rejection (`E1013`) on both compiler rungs.
//!
//! What it was for nine sprints: wolfc rejected it as `E1001` because
//! `for x in xs` MOVED `xs` and the push then read a moved place, which was
//! a fine answer arriving through the wrong lens (the reads-as-moves rule,
//! F-0005); lupin ran the program to completion, silently, with no trap at
//! all. One machine over-explained it and the other under-enforced it.
//!
//! s72's D40 settles it: iteration takes a read claim over the loop's whole
//! extent (`[mem.iter.excl]`), so `for` no longer moves its iterable and a
//! write during the loop is an exclusivity violation on its own terms. The
//! E1001 accident is structurally dead, and this file — which existed so the
//! divergence had a home in CI — becomes the witness that the divergence is
//! gone. It is a trap file now, and named like one (§13).

fn main() -> !int {
    var xs = List[int]()
    (mut xs).push(1)
    (mut xs).push(2)
    for x in xs {
        (mut xs).push(x)
    }
    0
}
